## Context

PR #781 (ticket 0444) re-scored the legacy census against the v2.1 reference
(173 plants) and corrected every census/RAG headline claim in `report.tex` —
*except* the per-model decomposition comparison table (`tab:decomposition`,
`report.tex` ~lines 428–447). That table is **hand-written** with v1-era F1
values, so it is now internally inconsistent with the v2 prose around it
(e.g. it still shows DeepSeek decomposed Méd. F1 = 87,3 % / best 98,8 %, while
the §rag_per_fuel prose two paragraphs later — and `\HeadlineMeanFOne` — now
say 68,6 % / best 76,5 %).

Two coupled problems:
1. **Stale values.** The five-model single-vs-decomposed grid carries v1 numbers.
2. **No wiring.** It is a hand-curated `tabular` in the manuscript, with no
   Makefile target deriving it from `measurements.jsonl`. This is exactly the
   class the project rule forbids (`rules/coding-python.md` § Build: "No
   hand-curated data in the pipeline. Every CSV/tex file referenced by slides or
   report must have a Makefile target that generates it from measurements.jsonl
   or another tracked source"). Re-typing v2 numbers by hand would re-arm the
   same staleness trap on the next reference bump.

`report.tex` is internal ("no future", the preprint is `slides/manuscript/
main.md`), which is why 0444 deferred this rather than blocking the merge — but
internal does not license a self-contradicting document, and the wiring fix is
the durable answer.

## Current table (v1, to be replaced)

`tab:decomposition` — columns: Modèle | Requête unique (Méd. F1, Meilleur) |
Décomposé (Méd. F1, Meilleur). Five rows: Mistral Small 4, GPT-5.4,
Mistral Large 3, DeepSeek V3.2, Gemini 2.5 Flash Lite. Bold marks the
decomposition winners.

## v2.1 source data (verify by running the script, do NOT hand-copy these)

Single query = `prompt_version='rag_extract'`; decomposed = `'rag_per_fuel'`
(NOT `rag_per_fuel_v2`, which is a *different* model generation — see wrinkle).
Surveyed 2026-06-08 from the merged v2 mart (median / best, n):

- mistral-small-2603 : single 0.515/0.620 (n=3) · per_fuel 0.545/0.607 (n=3)
- gpt-5.4            : single 0.713/0.713 (n=3) · per_fuel 0.655/0.659 (n=3)
- mistral-large-2512 : single 0.612/0.649 (n=3) · per_fuel 0.762/0.763 (n=3)
- deepseek-v3.2      : single 0.518/0.643 (n=3) · per_fuel 0.704/0.765 (n=4)
- gemini-2.5-flash-lite : single 0.465/0.496 (n=3) · per_fuel 0.540/0.646 (n=3)

These are hypotheses to confirm via the generated artifact, per
`rules/workflow.md` (AEDIST) § "Derive prose from generated artifacts" — the
adherence test below re-derives them independently.

### Wrinkle to resolve

The v1 table's "Décomposé" column mixes generations. `rag_per_fuel` is the
2026-05 decomposition run; `rag_per_fuel_v2` is a later sweep on *newer* model
slugs (gemini-3-flash-preview, kimi-k2.5, glm-4.7-flash, minimax-m2.7,
qwen3.5-122b). The five table models all have `rag_per_fuel` rows, so use that
for parity with "single query" on the *same* model. Decide explicitly whether
to keep the v2 table to these five (recommended, preserves the
single-vs-decomposed contrast) or expand it — document the membership choice in
the script, not by accident.

## Relevant files

- `report/report.tex` (~428–447) — replace the hand-written `tabular` with an
  `\input{inputs/generated/tab_decomposition}` (or append rows to an existing
  generated file — beware the tectonic `\InputIfFileExists` wrong-file trap,
  `rules/writing.md` (AEDIST) § LaTeX macro includes; a real generated target is
  fine, that rule warns against *empty* placeholder includes).
- `src/aedist/tabulate_decomposition.py` (NEW) — generator; mirror
  `tabulate_census.py` / `tabulate_status_difficulty.py` structure
  (argparse `--output`, load_metrics, group, emit LaTeX, log row count).
- `experiments/render.mk` — add a `$(ANALYSIS_GEN)/tab_decomposition.tex:
  $(ANALYSIS_MEASUREMENTS) <script>` rule and list it in the P3 aggregate
  alongside `tab_census.tex` / `tab_status_difficulty.tex`.
- `src/aedist/tabulate_macros.py::load_headline_result` — the n=4 DeepSeek
  per_fuel result is already the single source of truth for the §-text CI;
  reuse its filter semantics so the table's DeepSeek cell and the prose agree.
- `measurements.jsonl` — the v2 mart (source).

## Actions

1. Write `tabulate_decomposition.py`: for the five models, compute median + best
   F1 for `rag_extract` (single) and `rag_per_fuel` (decomposed) from
   load_metrics(); exclude SYNTHETIC_SUFFIXES; emit the LaTeX table with the
   same caption/label (`tab:decomposition`), bolding the per-model winner.
2. Wire the `render.mk` target + aggregate membership.
3. Replace the hand-written table in `report.tex` with the `\input`.
4. Add the adherence test (below).
5. `make -f experiments/render.mk report/inputs/generated/tab_decomposition.tex`,
   then `make report` — confirm it builds and the DeepSeek decomposed cell
   matches `\HeadlineMeanFOne` (68,6 %).

## Test (write first — red)

`tests/test_tabulate_decomposition.py`:
- `test_decomposition_table_matches_mart`: load_metrics(), independently compute
  median/best F1 for one model (e.g. deepseek-v3.2 rag_per_fuel) and assert the
  generated `tab_decomposition.tex` contains that value formatted — re-deriving
  from the artifact, not a hard-coded literal (guards against the
  re-typed-number trap; mirrors `test_tabulate_census.py`).
- `test_decomposition_no_v1_values`: assert the stale strings "98,8" and "94,8"
  (v1 DeepSeek/Mistral-Large best) do NOT appear in the generated file.

## Verification

- [ ] `grep -n 'tabular' report/report.tex` no longer matches inside the
      decomposition subsection (table is now `\input`ed).
- [ ] `tab_decomposition.tex` has a `render.mk` target with `$(ANALYSIS_MEASUREMENTS)`
      prerequisite; `make -f experiments/render.mk <target>` regenerates it.
- [ ] DeepSeek decomposed Méd. F1 cell == 68,6 % (== `\HeadlineMeanFOne`).
- [ ] `make report` builds; no "Missing $" / overfull errors from the new table.
- [ ] `pytest tests/test_tabulate_decomposition.py` green.

## Invariants

- Do not touch `main.md` — it carries no per-model decomposition table; this is
  report.tex-only.
- Keep `tab:decomposition` label/caption stable (any `\ref` to it must resolve).
- `make check` stays green; the new adherence test must catch a wrong value.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `tab:decomposition` is a generated artifact produced by a `render.mk`
      target from `measurements.jsonl`, `\input`ed by `report.tex`.
- [ ] Its values match the v2.1 mart (re-derived, not hand-typed) and are
      internally consistent with the surrounding v2 prose.
- [ ] Adherence test re-derives at least one cell from the mart and rejects the
      v1 values.
- [ ] `make report` builds; `make check` green.

